n for vegans is around 1  gram of protein per kilogram of body  weight a day compared to 0.8 grams of  protein per kilogram for non-vegans this  basically nets out to like a 14 gram  increase in protein for someone who's  vegan and weighs around 140 pounds  compared to a non-vegan of the same  weight we also want to try to pair that  plant-based protein with a source of  vitamin c from things like fruits and  vegetables to really help the absorption  which i've seen ashley do often but the  bottom line is as long as you're eating  enough total calories your food options  aren't heavily restricted like in the  case of a ketotarian or fruitarian diet  and you're switching up your protein  sources day to day there's really no  reason to worry your girl ashley seems  to have this down  mustard green crispy chicken orange  chicken  honestly tastes pretty good  i've never had a stuffed jalapeno this  is stuffed with like some type of like  protein or something it's good this is  kelly's absolute favorite thing i grew  up eating a lot of spanish because my  grandma would make it for me now i'm  eating the vegetarian version  that looks so good you know a lot of  people ask me if they can be vegan and  still eat intuitively and to that i say  oh yes intuitive eating is about  self-care it's not just about eating  whatever you want so when the intention  behind limiting animal products is not  related to changing your body and we're  giving ourselves unconditional  permission to enjoy let's say like a  vegan fried chicken or vegan sushi we  tend not to feel trapped in the diet  cycle of deprivation and denial  thankfully there are so many more vegan  options that make this paradigm shift  possible for folks who want to take it  on as long as these options still feel  satisfying to you and you're not  limiting yourself of the vegan  alternatives this is still of course  compatible with intuitive eating but in  ashley's meal specifically we're getting  some more protein variety with the  crispy chicken and the orange chicken  which according to the happy veggie  website are made with soy and wheat  protein the mustard greens also provide  a calcium and iron boost which are key  nutrients of concern on a vegan diet and  then of course we've got lots of fun  stuff like the jalapeno poppers dragon  rolls and most importantly a really fun  family style meal so lots of good stuff  to love what are my overall thoughts on  ashley's diet well based on our rough  calculations it looks like ashley's  consuming enough calories for her needs  and i'm seeing around a solid 90 grams  or so of protein from a wide variety of  protein sources plus lots of sources of  calcium iron and b12 so she clearly has  this plant-based nutrition thing down  and despite choosing a vegan diet i  never got any sense that she's  intentionally restricting in any way in  fact we watched a bunch of videos like  looking for red flags and unless we miss  something specific which is of course  always possible we obviously can't watch  everything we really didn't catch any  red flags  she seems to genuinely enjoy preparing  eating and sharing her meals with her  roomies which i think is awesome plus  all of her meals were super balanced and  easy to prepare and i love the  combination of homemade and takeaway  ingredients and meals overall i think  ashley is super fun low fuss and  definitely knows how to do a vegan diet  justice so on that note that is all that  i have for you guys today if you like  this video be sure to give it the thumbs  up leave me a comment below on who you'd  like to see me review next subscribe to  the channel and i'll see you next time  on abby's kitchen bye  [Music]

